        "## Extension: numpy vectorisation and pandas idioms\n",
        "\n",
        "This section is for members who already code or want to go further. Work on it independently and bring questions to the supported working time. For further reading, see [10 minutes to pandas](https://pandas.pydata.org/docs/user_guide/10min.html) and [NumPy: the absolute basics for beginners](https://numpy.org/doc/stable/user/absolute_beginners.html).\n",
        "\n",
        "**Vectorisation** means applying one operation to a whole array at once. numpy runs the loop in compiled code, which is much faster than a Python loop, and the code reads closer to the maths.\n",
        "\n",
        "If `p` is a numpy array of prices, `p[1:]` is every price from the second onwards and `p[:-1]` is every price up to the second to last. Dividing them element by element gives every return in one line."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "id": "aa87c84f",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "aa87c84f"
      },
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "# Worked example: time a Python loop against a vectorised calculation on a million prices.\n",
        "import time\n",
        "p = 100 * np.exp(np.cumsum(np.random.default_rng(1).normal(0, 0.01, 1_000_000)))\n",
        "\n",
        "start = time.perf_counter()\n",
        "loop_returns = [p[i] / p[i - 1] - 1 for i in range(1, len(p))]\n",
        "loop_time = time.perf_counter() - start\n",
        "\n",
        "start = time.perf_counter()\n",
        "vec_returns = p[1:] / p[:-1] - 1\n",
        "vec_time = time.perf_counter() - start\n",
        "\n",
        "print(f\"Loop: {loop_time:.3f} s   Vectorised: {vec_time:.4f} s   Speed-up: {loop_time / vec_time:.0f}x\")"
      ]
    },
